// JarvisLive.js — the /jarvis route (the folder name is legacy, not the URL).

// A public, no-sign-in page where a visitor talks to Jarvis: the terminal AI
// coding assistant from github.com/Laxman824/JarvisAssistant, running for real
// as a serverless function. Not a mockup and not a recording — the same
// agent loop, the same tools, answering out of its own source code.

// It exists because CAMSCode — the enterprise version of this, and the strongest
// project on the site — can never be demonstrated. This one can.

// SHAPE OF THE PAGE (revised 2026-08-08). It is a chat application: a fixed
// viewport, one scrolling transcript, a composer pinned to the floor. Each
// turn is user question → the trace that answered it → the answer. The earlier
// build put four paragraphs of framing above a single page-level terminal, and
// the framing pushed the actual product below the fold on arrival while the
// terminal ended up orphaned in the middle of long answers.

// So the framing now lives *inside* the transcript as its first block: fully
// present on arrival, scrolled away for good the moment a conversation starts.
// The long version is behind a disclosure — a recruiter who wants the why can
// open it; everyone else gets to the thing itself immediately.

// The trace is per-turn and inline, expanded on the newest turn and collapsed
// on older ones. Collapsing everything by default (as a normal chat app would)
// would hide the one thing this page exists to show.

// The badge in the composer always states which transport actually served the
// answer. That honesty is a feature: a "live" badge over a canned response
// would be a lie, and the whole point of the page is that this one is real.

// MOTION (added 2026-08-08). The page used to be static until you typed, which
// made a live agent look like a screenshot. Three things move now, and each one
// is telling the truth about something: the sample trace *plays itself* on
// arrival so the payoff is visible before you scroll; the composer types out
// what you could ask; and a turn in flight shows its own stopwatch and can be
// stopped. Everything here is gated on prefers-reduced-motion.

